{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01PTcQpcu9NkuVo9CWVNT9uW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Based on my searches, I've gathered sufficient information about CoRL reviewing standards. Let me consolidate what I know:\n\n- CoRL is a top robotics conference with acceptance rate around 30-35% historically\n- Review dimensions: originality, quality, clarity, significance, relevance, limitations\n- The review form typically uses a scale similar to other ML venues (1-10 overall rating)\n- CoRL 2024 had 671 submissions\n\nNow let me proceed with the full review. I need to dispatch two reviewers:\n- R1: Methods + Experiments\n- R2: Novelty + Related Work\n\nThen run the roundtable: Step 1 independent → Step 2 cross-share → Step 3 converge → Step 4 synthesize.\n\n\nI'll now have both reviewers conduct their independent assessments simultaneously.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1570,"cache_read_input_tokens":54884,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1570},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b215f82-0f5c-4b30-b56b-cf1e505ff256","uuid":"60713a51-3968-4101-b407-84c67a70b74e"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01PTcQpcu9NkuVo9CWVNT9uW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"CoRL 审稿标准已收集。CoRL 历史接受率约 30-35%，评审维度包括 originality、quality (soundness)、clarity、significance、relevance to robot learning。使用 1-10 整体评分，confidence 1-5。\n\n现在并行派遣两位 Reviewer 进行独立审稿。"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1570,"cache_read_input_tokens":54884,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1570},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b215f82-0f5c-4b30-b56b-cf1e505ff256","uuid":"66c25d5b-7dff-4c4d-969e-b2368959262e"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01PTcQpcu9NkuVo9CWVNT9uW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LPjFU9kEwtfi8UeALVd523","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**. \n\n## CoRL Review Standards\n- Venue: Conference on Robot Learning (CoRL), top robotics venue, ~30-35% acceptance rate\n- Dimensions: Originality, Quality/Soundness, Clarity, Significance, Relevance to robot learning\n- Overall rating scale: 1-10 (1=trivial/wrong, 10=top 5% seminal)\n- Confidence: 1-5 (5=absolutely certain)\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Rethinking VLA Safety Evaluation: When 98% of Collision Avoidance is an Illusion\"\n\nThe paper argues that collision avoidance rate (CAR)—the standard safety metric for VLA models—is systematically contaminated by \"fake safety\": models score high CAR not because they avoid collisions but because they don't move (action collapse) or follow incidentally safe paths. Three model families tested: Pi0 (LoRA+SDF finetuned, TSR=0%, CAR=91.5%, 99.6% fake safe), Pi0.5 baseline (TSR=73%, CAR=28%, 98.2% fake safe in safe episodes), OpenVLA zero-shot (TSR~8%, CAR~72%, ~70% fake safe).\n\nThey propose displacement-filtered CAR (dfCAR) that requires EE displacement >0.1m before counting an episode as safe. As a diagnostic probe, they train Pi0.5 with SDF auxiliary loss at λ∈{0, 0.005, 0.01, 0.1} to show real avoidance is achievable (+25pp CAR, 0% fake safety) but at extreme TSR cost (73%→5-9%). Phase-2 ablation (remove SDF after 10K steps) shows TSR recovery (→42%) but CAR regression (→32%), indicating safety requires continuous supervision. Cross-scene analysis: SDF effect attenuates on spatial_bowl (+5pp vs +25pp on object_pudding) when arm-body collisions dominate.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full planned experiments include:\n- A1 EE displacement re-eval (50ep) — confirming fake safety with EE displacement metric\n- OpenVLA zero-shot eval (200ep) — cross-model fake safety \n- LoRA baseline eval (2 seeds, 30K) — attribution decomposition (LoRA contributes +11pp, SDF +14.5pp)\n- Phase 2 λ=0 ablation — safety persistence test\n- spatial_bowl A1 training + eval — cross-scene generalization\n- Lambda=0.1 bracket — 4-point λ curve\n- Plan_011: Decision on whether full A1 re-eval needed\n\nThe paper appears to cover all these experiments.\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focused on **Methods** and **Experiments**. Address:\n\n1. **Methodological Soundness**\n   - Is dfCAR well-defined and justified? Is the 0.1m threshold arbitrary?\n   - Is SDF auxiliary loss properly formulated? How exactly is L_SDF computed?\n   - Is the phase-2 ablation design sound?\n   - Are there methodological gaps?\n\n2. **Experimental Rigor**\n   - Statistical significance: How many seeds? Error bars? The λ=0.1 experiment uses only 1 seed.\n   - Episode counts: 200 episodes for most, 50 for A1 re-eval. Is this sufficient?\n   - Are baselines appropriate? Missing baselines?\n   - Is the LoRA attribution analysis (Table 3-4) well-controlled? Confounds?\n   - Lambda curve: only 4 points, is this sufficient to characterize the tradeoff?\n\n3. **Experimental Coverage** (compare against review_context.yaml)\n   - Are all planned experiments reported? Any gaps?\n   - Quality of cross-model analysis (3 families but very different conditions: fine-tuned Pi0, fine-tuned Pi0.5, zero-shot OpenVLA)\n   - Cross-scene analysis limited to 2 scenes\n\n4. **Reproducibility**\n   - Are implementation details sufficient?\n   - Training details (steps, learning rates, LoRA rank, etc.)?\n\n5. **Presentation of Results**\n   - Are tables clear? I note that page 3 has significant formatting issues (Tables 1 and 3 overlap/are garbled)\n   - Figures mentioned but not visible in the PDF — lambda curve, scatter plots\n\nProvide your review in the following structured format:\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods & Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Bulleted list]\n\n### Weaknesses  \n[Bulleted list, each with severity: minor/moderate/major]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Missing Experiments / Suggestions\n[If any]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/4\n- Empirical rigor: X/4\n- Clarity (methods/tables): X/4\n- Overall: X/10\n- Confidence: X/5\n```"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1570,"cache_read_input_tokens":54884,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1570},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b215f82-0f5c-4b30-b56b-cf1e505ff256","uuid":"a589db65-0b32-4079-92d8-4ba6474623f5"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a8b63e60051167df0","tool_use_id":"toolu_01LPjFU9kEwtfi8UeALVd523","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**. \n\n## CoRL Review Standards\n- Venue: Conference on Robot Learning (CoRL), top robotics venue, ~30-35% acceptance rate\n- Dimensions: Originality, Quality/Soundness, Clarity, Significance, Relevance to robot learning\n- Overall rating scale: 1-10 (1=trivial/wrong, 10=top 5% seminal)\n- Confidence: 1-5 (5=absolutely certain)\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Rethinking VLA Safety Evaluation: When 98% of Collision Avoidance is an Illusion\"\n\nThe paper argues that collision avoidance rate (CAR)—the standard safety metric for VLA models—is systematically contaminated by \"fake safety\": models score high CAR not because they avoid collisions but because they don't move (action collapse) or follow incidentally safe paths. Three model families tested: Pi0 (LoRA+SDF finetuned, TSR=0%, CAR=91.5%, 99.6% fake safe), Pi0.5 baseline (TSR=73%, CAR=28%, 98.2% fake safe in safe episodes), OpenVLA zero-shot (TSR~8%, CAR~72%, ~70% fake safe).\n\nThey propose displacement-filtered CAR (dfCAR) that requires EE displacement >0.1m before counting an episode as safe. As a diagnostic probe, they train Pi0.5 with SDF auxiliary loss at λ∈{0, 0.005, 0.01, 0.1} to show real avoidance is achievable (+25pp CAR, 0% fake safety) but at extreme TSR cost (73%→5-9%). Phase-2 ablation (remove SDF after 10K steps) shows TSR recovery (→42%) but CAR regression (→32%), indicating safety requires continuous supervision. Cross-scene analysis: SDF effect attenuates on spatial_bowl (+5pp vs +25pp on object_pudding) when arm-body collisions dominate.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full planned experiments include:\n- A1 EE displacement re-eval (50ep) — confirming fake safety with EE displacement metric\n- OpenVLA zero-shot eval (200ep) — cross-model fake safety \n- LoRA baseline eval (2 seeds, 30K) — attribution decomposition (LoRA contributes +11pp, SDF +14.5pp)\n- Phase 2 λ=0 ablation — safety persistence test\n- spatial_bowl A1 training + eval — cross-scene generalization\n- Lambda=0.1 bracket — 4-point λ curve\n- Plan_011: Decision on whether full A1 re-eval needed\n\nThe paper appears to cover all these experiments.\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focused on **Methods** and **Experiments**. Address:\n\n1. **Methodological Soundness**\n   - Is dfCAR well-defined and justified? Is the 0.1m threshold arbitrary?\n   - Is SDF auxiliary loss properly formulated? How exactly is L_SDF computed?\n   - Is the phase-2 ablation design sound?\n   - Are there methodological gaps?\n\n2. **Experimental Rigor**\n   - Statistical significance: How many seeds? Error bars? The λ=0.1 experiment uses only 1 seed.\n   - Episode counts: 200 episodes for most, 50 for A1 re-eval. Is this sufficient?\n   - Are baselines appropriate? Missing baselines?\n   - Is the LoRA attribution analysis (Table 3-4) well-controlled? Confounds?\n   - Lambda curve: only 4 points, is this sufficient to characterize the tradeoff?\n\n3. **Experimental Coverage** (compare against review_context.yaml)\n   - Are all planned experiments reported? Any gaps?\n   - Quality of cross-model analysis (3 families but very different conditions: fine-tuned Pi0, fine-tuned Pi0.5, zero-shot OpenVLA)\n   - Cross-scene analysis limited to 2 scenes\n\n4. **Reproducibility**\n   - Are implementation details sufficient?\n   - Training details (steps, learning rates, LoRA rank, etc.)?\n\n5. **Presentation of Results**\n   - Are tables clear? I note that page 3 has significant formatting issues (Tables 1 and 3 overlap/are garbled)\n   - Figures mentioned but not visible in the PDF — lambda curve, scatter plots\n\nProvide your review in the following structured format:\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods & Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Bulleted list]\n\n### Weaknesses  \n[Bulleted list, each with severity: minor/moderate/major]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Missing Experiments / Suggestions\n[If any]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/4\n- Empirical rigor: X/4\n- Clarity (methods/tables): X/4\n- Overall: X/10\n- Confidence: X/5\n```","uuid":"308f3e0e-77e5-4b29-b691-d411aaccbd43","session_id":"3b215f82-0f5c-4b30-b56b-cf1e505ff256"}
