{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Mtfk81CfqkVdvNcnQJRFY1","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\appendix\n2\t\n3\t\\section{Displacement Threshold Sensitivity Analysis}\n4\t\\label{sec:appendix_threshold}\n5\t\n6\tTo validate the robustness of our displacement threshold $\\tau_d$, we evaluate the frozen rate across four orders of magnitude. Table~\\ref{tab:threshold_sensitivity} shows that the fake safety pattern persists across all reasonable threshold choices: A1-LoRA exhibits near-total action collapse regardless of threshold, while Pi0.5+A1 maintains 0\\% frozen rate even at the most conservative setting.\n7\t\n8\t\\begin{table}[h]\n9\t\\centering\n10\t\\caption{Frozen rate (\\% of episodes with $d_\\mathrm{max} < \\tau_d$) across threshold values.}\n11\t\\label{tab:threshold_sensitivity}\n12\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n13\t\\toprule\n14\t$\\tau_d$ & A1-LoRA (Pi0) & Pi0.5 Baseline & Pi0.5+A1 (SDF aux) \\\\\n15\t\\midrule\n16\t0.01 mm & \\placeholder{} & \\placeholder{} & \\placeholder{} \\\\\n17\t0.1 mm  & \\placeholder{} & \\placeholder{} & \\placeholder{} \\\\\n18\t1.0 mm  & \\placeholder{} & \\placeholder{} & \\placeholder{} \\\\\n19\t10 mm   & \\placeholder{} & \\placeholder{} & \\placeholder{} \\\\\n20\t\\bottomrule\n21\t\\end{tabular}\n22\t\\end{table}\n23\t\n24\tThe choice of $\\tau_d = 0.1$ mm represents a conservative threshold: end-effector displacement below this value is physically negligible in manipulation tasks where typical action magnitudes are 1--10 mm per step. Our conclusions hold for any threshold in the range $[0.01, 1.0]$ mm.\n25\t\n26\t\n27\t\\section{Per-Task Breakdown}\n28\t\\label{sec:appendix_pertask}\n29\t\n30\tTable~\\ref{tab:pertask_object} provides the full per-task decomposition for the object\\_pudding suite (Level I). Task difficulty varies substantially: Task 1 (chocolate\\_pudding) consistently shows the lowest CAR across all methods due to tight obstacle placement, while Task 0 (orange\\_juice) shows the highest CAR.\n31\t\n32\t\\begin{table}[h]\n33\t\\centering\n34\t\\caption{Per-task metrics on SafeLIBERO object\\_pudding Level I (50 episodes per task).}\n35\t\\label{tab:pertask_object}\n36\t\\begin{tabular}{llccc}\n37\t\\toprule\n38\tTask & Method & TSR (\\%) & CAR (\\%) & CAR$_\\text{f}$ (\\%) \\\\\n39\t\\midrule\n40\t\\multirow{3}{*}{T0: orange\\_juice}\n41\t & Pi0.5 Baseline & \\placeholder{} & \\placeholder{} & \\placeholder{} \\\\\n42\t & Pi0.5+A1 $\\lambda$=0.005 & \\placeholder{} & \\placeholder{} & \\placeholder{} \\\\\n43\t & Pi0.5+A1 $\\lambda$=0.01 & \\placeholder{} & \\placeholder{} & \\placeholder{} \\\\\n44\t\\midrule\n45\t\\multirow{3}{*}{T1: chocolate\\_pudding}\n46\t & Pi0.5 Baseline & \\placeholder{} & \\placeholder{} & \\placeholder{} \\\\\n47\t & Pi0.5+A1 $\\lambda$=0.005 & \\placeholder{} & \\placeholder{} & \\placeholder{} \\\\\n48\t & Pi0.5+A1 $\\lambda$=0.01 & \\placeholder{} & \\placeholder{} & \\placeholder{} \\\\\n49\t\\midrule\n50\t\\multirow{3}{*}{T2: milk}\n51\t & Pi0.5 Baseline & \\placeholder{} & \\placeholder{} & \\placeholder{} \\\\\n52\t & Pi0.5+A1 $\\lambda$=0.005 & \\placeholder{} & \\placeholder{} & \\placeholder{} \\\\\n53\t & Pi0.5+A1 $\\lambda$=0.01 & \\placeholder{} & \\placeholder{} & \\placeholder{} \\\\\n54\t\\midrule\n55\t\\multirow{3}{*}{T3: bbq\\_sauce}\n56\t & Pi0.5 Baseline & \\placeholder{} & \\placeholder{} & \\placeholder{} \\\\\n57\t & Pi0.5+A1 $\\lambda$=0.005 & \\placeholder{} & \\placeholder{} & \\placeholder{} \\\\\n58\t & Pi0.5+A1 $\\lambda$=0.01 & \\placeholder{} & \\placeholder{} & \\placeholder{} \\\\\n59\t\\bottomrule\n60\t\\end{tabular}\n61\t\\end{table}\n62\t\n63\t\n64\t\\section{Training Details}\n65\t\\label{sec:appendix_training}\n66\t\n67\t\\paragraph{Architecture.}\n68\tWe use LoRA~\\citep{hu2022lora} adapters injected into the PaliGemma vision encoder (rank 16, $\\alpha$=16) and the action expert (rank 32, $\\alpha$=32), following the configuration of OpenPi. The SDF decoder is a 4-layer MLP (hidden dimension 256) with skip connections, taking pooled suffix features (1024-d) and query points (3-d) as input. The end-effector extractor is a 2-layer MLP (1024 $\\to$ 256 $\\to$ 3).\n69\t\n70\t\\paragraph{Training schedule.}\n71\tTraining proceeds in two phases:\n72\t\\begin{itemize}\n73\t    \\item \\textbf{Phase 1} (steps 0--5000): Backbone and LoRA parameters are frozen. Only the SDF decoder and EE extractor are trained, allowing them to learn meaningful representations before the policy adapts.\n74\t    \\item \\textbf{Phase 2} (steps 5000--30000): LoRA parameters are unfrozen with a 100-step gradient warmup ramp. All auxiliary heads continue training jointly with the policy.\n75\t\\end{itemize}\n76\t\n77\t\\paragraph{Hyperparameters.}\n78\t\\begin{table}[h]\n79\t\\centering\n80\t\\caption{Training hyperparameters.}\n81\t\\label{tab:hyperparams}\n82\t\\begin{tabular}{ll}\n83\t\\toprule\n84\tParameter & Value \\\\\n85\t\\midrule\n86\tBase model & Pi0.5 (OpenPi, $\\sim$3.5B parameters) \\\\\n87\tOptimizer & AdamW \\\\\n88\tLearning rate & $2.5 \\times 10^{-5}$ (cosine decay to $\\mathrm{lr}/10$) \\\\\n89\tWarmup steps & 1000 \\\\\n90\tBatch size & 32 \\\\\n91\tTotal steps & 30000 \\\\\n92\t$\\lambda_\\mathrm{sdf}$ & \\{0.005, 0.01\\} \\\\\n93\t$\\lambda_\\mathrm{ee}$ & 0.01 \\\\\n94\tSDF query points per step & 64 \\\\\n95\tSDF clamp $\\delta$ & 0.05 m \\\\\n96\tEikonal weight & 0.1 \\\\\n97\tLoRA rank (vision) & 16 \\\\\n98\tLoRA rank (action) & 32 \\\\\n99\tPhase 1 duration & 5000 steps \\\\\n100\t\\bottomrule\n101\t\\end{tabular}\n102\t\\end{table}\n103\t\n104\t\\paragraph{Data.}\n105\tWe use the SafeLIBERO object\\_pudding suite with pre-computed SDF ground truth. SDF values are computed offline from the obstacle mesh using a dense $64^3$ voxel grid. During training, 64 query points are randomly sampled per step from this grid.\n106\t\n107\t\\paragraph{Computational resources.}\n108\tAll training experiments are conducted on NVIDIA \\placeholder{GPU model} GPUs with \\placeholder{XX}\\,GB memory. Phase~1 (5K steps) takes approximately \\placeholder{X} hours on a single GPU; Phase~2 (25K steps) takes approximately \\placeholder{X} hours. Evaluation of 200 episodes requires approximately \\placeholder{X} minutes. Total computational budget for the experiments reported in this paper is approximately \\placeholder{X} GPU-hours.\n109\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LPjFU9kEwtfi8UeALVd523","session_id":"3b215f82-0f5c-4b30-b56b-cf1e505ff256","uuid":"d9a32fa6-6305-46ab-9179-c5e553a60c2d","timestamp":"2026-05-25T23:12:59.290Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Q66fr2EkcE5jvD6Pt8ofrT","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Discussion and Conclusion}\\label{sec:conclusion}\n2\t\n3\tThis work identifies a systematic contamination in VLA collision avoidance evaluation: standard CAR conflates genuine obstacle avoidance with inaction caused by action collapse. Across all Pi0-family configurations evaluated on SafeLIBERO, over 98\\% of episodes classified as safe exhibit sub-threshold displacement, rendering raw CAR unreliable as a safety metric. Displacement-filtered CAR (CAR$_\\text{f}$) separates these two behaviors by excluding episodes below a displacement threshold $\\tau_d$, providing a metric that credits only active avoidance. Two structural findings reinforce this diagnosis: TSR and CAR are anti-correlated under current methods (Section~\\ref{sec:tsr_car}), and end-effector-only SDF refinement fails to prevent full-body collisions even with ground-truth geometry (Section~\\ref{sec:ee_only}). Training-time SDF supervision on Pi0.5 is the sole configuration that produces non-zero CAR$_\\text{f}$ with zero frozen rate, placing it in a region of the TSR-CAR plane that no other evaluated method occupies (Section~\\ref{sec:a1_pi05}).\n4\t\n5\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n6\tThe empirical scope of this study is bounded in several respects. All experiments use the \\texttt{object\\_pudding} Level~I suite from SafeLIBERO (4 tasks). The fake safety mechanism is model-agnostic in principle: any action-collapsed VLA will receive inflated CAR. Direct verification, however, covers only the Pi0 and Pi0.5 model families; whether the same contamination pattern appears in OpenVLA, RT-2, or other VLA architectures requires additional experiments. Preliminary evaluation on the \\texttt{spatial\\_bowl} suite confirms this pattern: all Pi0 variants achieve 0\\% CAR despite substantial end-effector displacement (0.2--0.3\\,m), consistent with arm-body collisions dominating the failure mode. Pi0.5 achieves only 13\\% CAR on this suite, suggesting that spatial tasks with narrow corridors pose additional challenges beyond those captured by the \\texttt{object\\_pudding} benchmark. The EE-only SDF oracle (Section~\\ref{sec:ee_only}) tests end-effector-level refinement exclusively; full-body SDF monitoring along the kinematic chain may yield different outcomes. The displacement threshold $\\tau_d = 0.1$\\,mm is empirically robust across four orders of magnitude (Appendix~\\ref{sec:appendix_threshold}), but remains task-dependent in principle. Pi0.5+A1 results are reported at the 10K-step checkpoint (seed~0); the 30K checkpoint is pending, and the final magnitude of the TSR-CAR tradeoff is not yet determined. In narrow-corridor scenarios (Task~3), SDF gradients redistribute collisions between obstacles rather than eliminate them: the point-wise gradient pushes the end-effector away from the nearest surface, but in bilateral obstacle geometries this shifts the trajectory into the opposing obstacle (Section~\\ref{sec:a1_pi05}). This collision transfer effect defines a boundary condition for training-time SDF supervision.\n7\t\n8\t\\paragraph{Future work.}\n9\tFour directions follow from this analysis. First, full-body SDF monitoring along the robot kinematic chain would address the structural gap identified in Section~\\ref{sec:ee_only}. Second, evaluation across additional SafeLIBERO suites and other manipulation benchmarks would test whether the contamination patterns generalize beyond the task family studied here. Third, cross-architecture evaluation on non-Pi0-family VLAs (OpenVLA, RT-2) would determine the prevalence of fake safety in the broader VLA landscape. Fourth, we recommend that future VLA safety evaluations report both raw CAR and CAR$_\\text{f}$; the gap between the two quantifies the degree of action-collapse contamination in a given model.\n10\t\n11\t\\paragraph{Broader impact.}\n12\tMetric reliability is a prerequisite for safe deployment of autonomous manipulation systems. Contaminated evaluation metrics create a risk that models with no genuine collision avoidance capability pass safety benchmarks and proceed to deployment. Displacement filtering is a low-cost correction: it requires only end-effector position logging, no additional data collection or model modification. The analysis in this work is conducted entirely in simulation; real-world deployment introduces additional safety considerations (sensor noise, sim-to-real transfer, dynamic obstacles) that fall outside the present scope.\n13\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019h1p5pE9JDM9Q3AeMPJ3dn","session_id":"3b215f82-0f5c-4b30-b56b-cf1e505ff256","uuid":"8c36e17b-357e-4566-bea4-1bf7b57423e1","timestamp":"2026-05-25T23:12:58.932Z"}
