{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01M9HpBAuLvw5Azh59Q6FepE","type":"tool_result","content":"docs/paper/experimental_framework.tex:36:\\item \\textit{Pi0.5 + A1}: The A1 architecture applied to the Pi0.5 backbone. Training proceeds in two phases: Phase~1 (steps 0--5K) trains only the auxiliary heads on detached features with the backbone frozen; Phase~2 (steps 5K--30K) unfreezes LoRA parameters for joint optimization. We evaluate $\\lambda_\\text{SDF} \\in \\{0.005, 0.01\\}$.\ndocs/paper/_archive/experiments.tex:20:\\paragraph{Training details.} All variants train for 30K steps with batch size 32 on the same dataset. Phase 1 (steps 0--5K) trains only the SDF decoder and EE extractor on detached features; Phase 2 (steps 5K--30K) unfreezes LoRA parameters (${\\sim}$33.5M) with linear gradient warmup over 100 steps. The $\\pi_0$-LoRA baseline skips Phase 1 and trains for 30K steps with $\\mathcal{L}_\\mathrm{action}$ only. Hyperparameters follow Section~\\ref{sec:training}.\ndocs/paper/_archive/method.tex:46:[Omitted long matching line]\ndocs/paper/_archive/method.tex:93:We adopt a two-phase training protocol that ensures SDF prediction accuracy before engaging gradient-guided refinement.\ndocs/paper/_archive/method.tex:97:\\paragraph{Phase 2: Joint training ($5\\mathrm{K} \\to 30\\mathrm{K}$ steps).} LoRA parameters are unfrozen with a linear gradient warmup over 100 steps. We consider three training variants that differ only in their loss composition:\ndocs/paper/_archive/method.tex:108:with $\\lambda_\\mathrm{SDF} = 0.1$ and $\\lambda_\\mathrm{EE} = 0.01$. Gradients flow from the auxiliary losses through the pooled features into the LoRA adapters, encouraging the action expert to develop features that encode workspace geometry.\ndocs/paper/_archive/method.tex:114:with $\\lambda_\\mathrm{refine} = 0.1$. The refinement loss is computed only when near-collision samples exist in the batch ($|\\mathcal{S}| > 0$), adding zero overhead for collision-free steps.\ndocs/paper/appendix.tex:74:    \\item \\textbf{Phase 2} (steps 5000--30000): LoRA parameters are unfrozen with a 100-step gradient warmup ramp. All auxiliary heads continue training jointly with the policy.\ndocs/paper/appendix.tex:108:All training experiments are conducted on NVIDIA \\placeholder{GPU model} GPUs with \\placeholder{XX}\\,GB memory. Phase~1 (5K steps) takes approximately \\placeholder{X} hours on a single GPU; Phase~2 (25K steps) takes approximately \\placeholder{X} hours. Evaluation of 200 episodes requires approximately \\placeholder{X} minutes. Total computational budget for the experiments reported in this paper is approximately \\placeholder{X} GPU-hours.\ndocs/paper/main.aux:122:\\@writefile{lof}{\\contentsline {figure}{\\numberline {4}{\\ignorespaces Effect of SDF loss weight $\\lambda $ on CAR and TSR for Pi0.5+A1. Four points: $\\lambda \\in \\{0, 0.005, 0.01, 0.1\\}$. The $\\lambda = 0$ point corresponds to the Pi0.5 baseline (LoRA only, no SDF loss). Pending: 3-seed results at 30K steps.}}{16}{figure.4}\\protected@file@percent }\ndocs/paper/main.aux:123:\\newlabel{fig:lambda_car}{{4}{16}{Effect of SDF loss weight $\\lambda $ on CAR and TSR for Pi0.5+A1. Four points: $\\lambda \\in \\{0, 0.005, 0.01, 0.1\\}$. The $\\lambda = 0$ point corresponds to the Pi0.5 baseline (LoRA only, no SDF loss). Pending: 3-seed results at 30K steps}{figure.4}{}}\ndocs/paper/analysis_claim4.tex:44:\\caption{Effect of SDF loss weight $\\lambda$ on CAR and TSR for Pi0.5+A1. Four points: $\\lambda \\in \\{0, 0.005, 0.01, 0.1\\}$. The $\\lambda = 0$ point corresponds to the Pi0.5 baseline (LoRA only, no SDF loss). Pending: 3-seed results at 30K steps.}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LPjFU9kEwtfi8UeALVd523","session_id":"3b215f82-0f5c-4b30-b56b-cf1e505ff256","uuid":"690d3f65-a148-47bb-ad41-ef337b5434cb","timestamp":"2026-05-25T23:13:10.902Z"}
