{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GWf2jJJW48aXjXVNCGCBs6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HrkQAhj8knZvkKHc4V7d2Z","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515/projects/diff_collision_sdf/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260525T223036/median/paper.tex","limit":100},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5732,"cache_read_input_tokens":51931,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5732,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019h1p5pE9JDM9Q3AeMPJ3dn","session_id":"3b215f82-0f5c-4b30-b56b-cf1e505ff256","uuid":"bb91f258-6b66-40a7-b064-7d5494b11b4b"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HrkQAhj8knZvkKHc4V7d2Z","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[10pt,twocolumn]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n4\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n5\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n6\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n7\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n8\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n9\t\n10\t\\title{Rethinking VLA Safety Evaluation:\\\\When 98\\% of Collision Avoidance is an Illusion}\n11\t\n12\t\\author{\n13\t  Anonymous Authors\n14\t}\n15\t\n16\t\\begin{document}\n17\t\\maketitle\n18\t\n19\t%==============================================================================\n20\t\\begin{abstract}\n21\tCollision avoidance rate (CAR) is the de facto safety metric for vision-language-action (VLA) models in manipulation benchmarks. We demonstrate that CAR is \\emph{systematically contaminated}: on SafeLIBERO, a fine-tuned Pi0 model achieves 91.5\\% CAR while 99.6\\% of its ``safe'' episodes exhibit obstacle displacement below 0.1\\,mm---the robot is frozen, not avoiding collisions. We term this \\emph{fake safety} and show it manifests across three model families (Pi0, Pi0.5, OpenVLA) with varying severity. To distinguish genuine avoidance from metric artifacts, we propose \\emph{displacement-filtered CAR}, which conditions on meaningful end-effector motion. As a diagnostic probe, we train Pi0.5 with an SDF auxiliary loss at varying strengths ($\\lambda \\in \\{0, 0.005, 0.01, 0.1\\}$) and demonstrate that geometric supervision produces \\emph{real} collision avoidance (+25\\,pp CAR, 0\\% fake safety, EE displacement $\\sim$0.5\\,m) but at severe task performance cost (TSR: 73\\%$\\to$5--9\\%). A phase-2 ablation removing SDF supervision shows task recovery (TSR$\\to$42\\%) but complete safety regression (CAR$\\to$32\\%), indicating that safety encoding requires continuous geometric supervision. Cross-scene analysis reveals that end-effector-only SDF attenuates sharply when arm-body collisions dominate (+5\\,pp vs.\\ +25\\,pp). Our findings establish that current VLA safety metrics are unreliable and that naive geometric supervision, while producing genuine avoidance, introduces an extreme capability--safety tradeoff demanding new training paradigms.\n22\t\\end{abstract}\n23\t\n24\t%==============================================================================\n25\t\\section{Introduction}\n26\t\\label{sec:intro}\n27\t\n28\tSafe deployment of vision-language-action (VLA) models requires reliable collision avoidance metrics. The dominant metric---collision avoidance rate (CAR)---counts the fraction of episodes with obstacle displacement below a threshold (typically 0.1\\,mm). High CAR is interpreted as evidence that the policy actively avoids obstacles.\n29\t\n30\tWe challenge this interpretation. Through large-scale evaluation (6000+ episodes across three model families), we identify two systematic failure modes that inflate CAR:\n31\t\n32\t\\begin{enumerate}\n33\t    \\item \\textbf{Action collapse fake safety}: Models with near-zero task success rate (TSR) achieve high CAR trivially---if the robot barely moves, it cannot collide. A fine-tuned Pi0 achieves 91.5\\% CAR with TSR=0\\% and median obstacle displacement $\\sim$5\\,nm.\n34\t    \\item \\textbf{Geometric path safety}: Capable models (Pi0.5, TSR=73\\%) that happen to follow collision-free trajectories score well on CAR without any avoidance \\emph{behavior}---they succeed at the task via paths that incidentally miss obstacles.\n35\t\\end{enumerate}\n36\t\n37\tNeither mode reflects intentional collision avoidance. We propose \\emph{displacement-filtered CAR} that requires minimum end-effector displacement ($>$0.1\\,m) before counting an episode as ``safely completed,'' thereby filtering frozen and trivially safe episodes.\n38\t\n39\tTo validate that real collision avoidance is detectable under our filtered metric, we train Pi0.5 with signed distance field (SDF) auxiliary losses at varying strengths. The SDF-trained models exhibit large EE displacements ($\\sim$0.5\\,m), zero fake safety, and genuine CAR improvement---confirming our metric distinguishes real from fake safety. However, this comes at extreme task performance cost, revealing a fundamental tension between geometric safety supervision and task execution in current VLA architectures.\n40\t\n41\tOur contributions:\n42\t\\begin{itemize}\n43\t    \\item Systematic identification of fake safety contamination in CAR across three VLA families.\n44\t    \\item Displacement-filtered CAR as a corrected evaluation metric.\n45\t    \\item SDF auxiliary loss as a diagnostic probe establishing that real avoidance is achievable but at extreme tradeoff.\n46\t    \\item A 4-point $\\lambda$ curve and phase-2 ablation characterizing the safety--capability landscape.\n47\t\\end{itemize}\n48\t\n49\t%==============================================================================\n50\t\\section{Related Work}\n51\t\n52\t\\paragraph{VLA Safety Evaluation.}\n53\tSafeLIBERO~\\cite{safelibero} introduced collision avoidance rate as a benchmark metric for manipulation safety. Subsequent work adopts CAR as a ground-truth indicator without examining whether high scores reflect genuine avoidance behavior. Our work reveals that CAR is systematically contaminated by action collapse and geometric path effects.\n54\t\n55\t\\paragraph{Collision Avoidance in Manipulation.}\n56\tClassical approaches use planning-time obstacle representations (signed distance fields, configuration-space obstacles) for collision-free motion planning. Recent learning-based methods integrate safety constraints into policy optimization. We use SDF not as a deployment solution but as a diagnostic training signal to produce verifiably real avoidance, enabling metric validation.\n57\t\n58\t\\paragraph{Safety--Performance Tradeoffs.}\n59\tThe tension between task completion and safety constraints is well-studied in control theory (Hamilton-Jacobi reachability, control barrier functions). We provide the first empirical characterization of this tradeoff in VLA fine-tuning, showing that naive geometric supervision produces extreme imbalance.\n60\t\n61\t%==============================================================================\n62\t\\section{Method}\n63\t\\label{sec:method}\n64\t\n65\t\\subsection{Displacement-Filtered CAR}\n66\t\n67\tStandard CAR counts episode $i$ as safe if obstacle displacement $d_i^{\\text{obs}} < \\epsilon$ (typically $\\epsilon = 0.1$\\,mm). We define \\emph{displacement-filtered CAR} (dfCAR):\n68\t\\begin{equation}\n69\t    \\text{dfCAR} = \\frac{|\\{i : d_i^{\\text{obs}} < \\epsilon \\;\\wedge\\; d_i^{\\text{EE}} > \\delta\\}|}{|\\{i : d_i^{\\text{EE}} > \\delta\\}|}\n70\t\\end{equation}\n71\twhere $d_i^{\\text{EE}}$ is end-effector displacement and $\\delta = 0.1$\\,m filters frozen episodes. Episodes with $d_i^{\\text{EE}} \\leq \\delta$ are excluded from both numerator and denominator, preventing action-collapsed policies from inflating the metric.\n72\t\n73\t\\subsection{Fake Safety Classification}\n74\t\n75\tWe classify an episode as \\emph{fake safe} if it satisfies:\n76\t\\begin{enumerate}\n77\t    \\item $d_i^{\\text{obs}} < 0.1$\\,mm (counted as ``safe'' by standard CAR), and\n78\t    \\item $d_i^{\\text{EE}} < 0.1$\\,m (robot did not meaningfully move).\n79\t\\end{enumerate}\n80\tThe \\emph{fake safe rate} is the fraction of standard-CAR-safe episodes that are fake safe.\n81\t\n82\t\\subsection{SDF Auxiliary Loss as Diagnostic Probe}\n83\t\n84\tTo generate policies with verifiable real avoidance, we augment LoRA fine-tuning of Pi0.5 with an SDF auxiliary loss:\n85\t\\begin{equation}\n86\t    \\mathcal{L} = \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{action}} + \\lambda \\cdot \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{SDF}}\n87\t\\end{equation}\n88\twhere $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{SDF}}$ penalizes predicted end-effector positions that are close to obstacle surfaces (low SDF values). We sweep $\\lambda \\in \\{0, 0.005, 0.01, 0.1\\}$ to characterize the dose--response relationship between geometric supervision strength and the safety--capability tradeoff.\n89\t\n90\t\\subsection{Phase-2 Ablation: Supervision Persistence}\n91\t\n92\tTo test whether SDF-induced safety persists without continuous supervision, we implement a two-phase protocol:\n93\t\\begin{enumerate}\n94\t    \\item Phase 1: Train with $\\lambda = 0.005$ for 10K steps (establishing safety encoding).\n95\t    \\item Phase 2: Resume training with $\\lambda = 0$ for 20K steps (removing supervision).\n96\t\\end{enumerate}\n97\tIf safety persists in Phase 2, geometric supervision could be used as a ``safety imprinting'' curriculum. If not, continuous supervision is required.\n98\t\n99\t%==============================================================================\n100\t\\section{Experiments}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019h1p5pE9JDM9Q3AeMPJ3dn","session_id":"3b215f82-0f5c-4b30-b56b-cf1e505ff256","uuid":"9488726d-bfa1-4220-aa6a-0c1548f822be","timestamp":"2026-05-25T23:13:23.884Z"}
